# garb cross-bpp at REAL call granularity (single row) — rejection CONFIRMED
date: 2026-07-28
host: Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
build: release, NO -C target-cpu=native
bench: cargo bench --features experimental --bench swizzle -- 'row_'

## Why re-measure

The original rejection (benchmarks/neon_crossbpp_sweep_2026-07-28.meta) used
whole-image buffers: 64x64, 256x256, 1024x1024, 4096x4096. That is NOT how
these are called. Consumers call them PER ROW — zenpixels-convert's
`convert_kernels.rs`:

    garb::bytes::rgb_to_bgra(&src[..width * 3], &mut dst[..width * 4])

inside a row loop, and garb ships 277 strided entry points for exactly that
pattern. A 1920-px RGB row is 5,760 bytes; the original sweep's smallest case
was 12,288 bytes and its largest 67 MB — 100 to 10,000x larger than a real call.

This re-check exists because the same mistake INVERTED a verdict in zensim the
same day: `fused_blur_h_ssim` read 0.70x (NEON losing) at a synthetic
1024x1024 r=3, and 1.56x (NEON winning) at the instrumented shape the pipeline
actually uses. Any rejection measured at an unverified shape is suspect until
re-measured at the real one.

## Result — the rejection HOLDS at real granularity

| op / row width          | NEON     | scalar   | ratio |
|-------------------------|----------|----------|-------|
| rgb_to_bgra  /  256     |  17.7ns  |  13.2ns  | 0.75x |
| rgb_to_bgra  /  640     |  41.5ns  |  30.9ns  | 0.75x |
| rgb_to_bgra  / 1920     | 120.7ns  |  90.9ns  | 0.75x |
| rgb_to_bgra  / 3840     | 244.4ns  | 190.2ns  | 0.78x |
| rgba_to_rgb  /  256     |  27.5ns  |  26.8ns  | 0.98x |
| rgba_to_rgb  /  640     |  68.8ns  |  67.2ns  | 0.98x |
| rgba_to_rgb  / 1920     | 207.1ns  | 202.9ns  | 0.98x |
| rgba_to_rgb  / 3840     | 414.2ns  | 408.2ns  | 0.99x |

`rgb_to_bgra` is 33% SLOWER with the NEON kernel, consistently at all four real
row widths. `rgba_to_rgb` is a tie. Shipping these would make garb slower on
every ARM machine for its actual usage pattern.

Unlike zensim's fused_blur, correcting the shape did NOT reverse the verdict —
it reproduced it at a granularity that is 100-10,000x smaller, which is
stronger evidence than the original sweep. The kernels stay unshipped and
remain in `git stash` ("refuted: explicit vld3q/vld4q cross-bpp NEON kernels").

## Bench kept

`bench_crossbpp_row_sweep` in benches/swizzle.rs is committed so future
cross-bpp work is measured at the granularity consumers actually use, not only
at whole-image sizes. Both sweeps now exist; read the row one first.
